* Thu Oct 01 2026 Jeremy Melanson <1080872+zish@users.noreply.github.com> - 1.1.0-1
- Read the radio directly over nl80211 for the four things NetworkManager
  cannot answer, via a new Nl80211.cpp that talks raw AF_NETLINK and adds no
  runtime dependency at all -- libnl is not linked and the binary still needs
  exactly the six libraries 1.0.1 did (docs/60).
- Report the NEGOTIATED PHY in getConnectionCapabilities, so Android's "Wi-Fi
  standard" stops reading UNKNOWN. The PHY comes from which MCS attribute the
  driver sent on NL80211_STA_INFO_TX_BITRATE, which is the negotiated one by
  construction; NM's only candidate was the AP's advertised MaxBitrate, which
  measured 1170 Mb/s on a 20 MHz 2.4 GHz channel and so cannot be inverted into
  a PHY. The enum mapping was disassembled from this image's own
  service-wifi.jar, not assumed.
- Answer getDeviceWiphyCapabilities instead of returning null, and this is what
  makes the PHY work reach anything: ThroughputPredictor.predictThroughput logs
  "Null device capabilities passed to throughput predictor" and returns 0 as its
  FIRST act, before looking at the PHY, the width or the streams. Observed
  firing under 1.0.1 and absent since. Max Supported Tx/Rx Link speed go from
  -1 to real values, which the framework can only get from here.
- Report the RX bitrate separately from TX. NM exposes one Bitrate property, so
  rx had always been reported as tx -- 130 Mb/s for both where the live link was
  39 tx and 72.2 rx.
- Take RSSI from NL80211_STA_INFO_SIGNAL rather than inverting NM's 0..100
  quality. The 1.0.1 inverse is exact and stays as the fallback, but NM polls
  the associated AP on its own schedule and measured 3 dB low across five
  consecutive samples; Android now agrees with the driver's dBm on 4 of 4.
- Report real spatial-stream counts instead of a hardcoded 1x1, which had
  understated this 2x2 card by half.
- Report real packet counters in getPacketCounters. Correct but unexercised:
  Android only asks around a link probe, and SendMgmtFrame has never been
  called in this daemon's entire journal on this host. The tx_good/tx_bad
  columns in dumpsys come from getLinkLayerStats, which needs a vendor HAL this
  daemon does not serve, so those stay zero.
- Close the data race docs/59 left open as a design question. mIfname, mDevPath
  and mSelectorMac are now guarded by a recursive mutex, and the nl80211 path is
  keyed by an atomic ifindex so it shares no mutable state at all. A mutex and
  not a post-to-the-main-loop handoff, because the main loop itself calls into
  Android and waiting on it from a binder thread is a cycle with no timeout.

* Wed Sep 30 2026 Jeremy Melanson <1080872+zish@users.noreply.github.com> - 1.0.1-1
- Fix the RSSI Android is told. NmBackend converted NetworkManager's 0..100
  Strength with quality/2 - 100, which assumes NM's range is -100..-50 dBm; it
  is -100..-40, so the reported value was low by quality/10 dB -- 0 dB at the
  bottom of the scale and 10 dB at the top. The exact inverse is
  -100 + 3*quality/5, verified against nl80211 on 13 same-scan observations and
  across all 61 dBm values NM can represent (docs/59).
- Not cosmetic: this image's ScoringParams are rssi2=-83:-80:-73:-60, so a link
  1 dB shy of "good" was being reported 1.5 dB above "insufficient", and the
  framework's own score history fades that far every few seconds.
- Report channelBandwidth in getConnectionCapabilities, from NM's
  AccessPoint.Bandwidth. technology stays UNKNOWN deliberately: NM exposes only
  the AP's advertised capability, not the negotiated PHY. The parcel layout is
  unchanged, which matters -- this is the handler whose missing non-null marker
  killed system_server on every association in Stage 4.
- Cache the channel width from the host link event rather than calling the
  backend from a binder thread, which would race NmBackend::wifiDevicePath()'s
  writes to mDevPath and mIfname against the GLib main loop.

* Wed Sep 16 2026 Jeremy Melanson <1080872+zish@users.noreply.github.com> - 1.0.0-1
- Split out of waydroid-wifid.spec as its own source package (docs/47).
- waydroid-wifi-sync and its timer move to waydroid-ext-wifi-sync, so a
  credential-reconciler fix no longer reissues the daemon.
